  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "hd0uBFQAsllpgA6kH7tP+BXV7ZM=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:07.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`@powerhousedao/document-engineering` exposes several entry points, and picking the wrong one is a common early stumble because the components have overlapping names.\n\n**`/scalars`** holds the form tier. Each component corresponds to a GraphQL scalar type \u2014 `StringField`, `BooleanField`, `NumberField`, `PHIDField`, `AmountField`, `CurrencyCodeField` (note: there is **no** `CurrencyField` \u2014 that identifier has zero hits in the repo; the real components are `currency-code-field` and `amount-field`) \u2014 and they are built on react-hook-form. They ship out-of-the-box validation, and the price is that **they must be wrapped in the `Form` component from the same package** or they will not work properly. Even a single checkbox needs the wrapper:\n\n```tsx\nimport { Form, BooleanField } from \"@powerhousedao/document-engineering/scalars\";\n\nexport const Checkbox = ({ value, onChange }: CheckboxProps) => (\n  <Form onSubmit={() => {}}>\n    <BooleanField name=\"checked\" description=\"Mark as complete\" value={value} onChange={onChange} />\n  </Form>\n);\n```\n\nNote the `onSubmit={() => {}}` \u2014 in a Powerhouse editor there is no form submission, because changes go out as dispatched actions on change. The `Form` is there purely to provide the react-hook-form context.\n\n**`/ui`** holds the general-purpose tier: simplified variants of the scalar components that need no `Form` wrapper but correspondingly lack the built-in validation, plus components with no scalar counterpart at all \u2014 `Dropdown`, `Tooltip`, `Sidebar`, `Button`, `Icon` and more (`src/ui/components/`). This is where you go for layout and interaction rather than data entry.\n\n**`/table`** is its own tier, and this is where `ObjectSetTable` actually lives (`src/table/index.ts` re-exports it from `./table/object-set-table.js`) \u2014 **not** under `/ui`, as is easy to assume.\n\nThe complete export surface in `package.json` is: `.`, `./scalars`, `./scalars/*`, `./ui`, `./ui/*`, `./table`, `./table/*`, `./graphql`, `./graphql/*`, `./graphql-scalars`, `./graphql-scalars/*`, and `./style.css`. `./graphql` maps to `src/scalars/graphql/`, the scalar definitions themselves. Inside the repo the same surfaces are reachable via import maps `#assets`, `#scalars`, `#ui` and `#graphql`.\n\nThe practical rule: reach for `/scalars` when the component *is* a document-model field and you want validation for free; reach for `/ui` when you are composing an interface. And treat the component's source as the final word on props \u2014 Storybook documents common props but may lag or omit some.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/05-EditorsUI/00-DocumentEngineering.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._\n\n_Source-verified 2026-08-13 against the document-engineering repo at v1.40.5 (`/home/p/Powerhouse/document-engineering`), a separate repo from the powerhouse monorepo._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:35:07.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:07.000Z"
    }
  },
